A: Pex is a very powerful tool, but it cannot guarantee 100% code coverage. Particularly for public contracts where return types and exception handling are involved. It's possible that your specific case is not being fully covered. 
As an example, the Add method might return an integer representing the new count of elements, and if the Add method is public Pex should be able to find some coverage, but it might not account for if the collection is full, which is a contract your assertions can check.
When this happens, you have a couple of options. 


*

*As you suggested, use conditions for assertions to make sure you test the edge case that Pex doesn't cover.

*Create unit tests for all edge cases that Pex cannot find coverage. 

*Increase the MaxDiscoverTestCount and/or MaxStaticDrivingIterationCount settings to increase the number of Pex tests Pex produces.


Hope this helps. You can always check the Pex settings by opening the NUnit settings and searching for Pex.
Q: How to handle multiple versions of data with Cassandra We're considering using Cassandra as our data store and we've come across the need to handle multiple versions of data.
Does Cassandra support this use case natively, or do we need to write custom code to accomplish this behavior? 

A: Cassandra supports this via the timestamp feature: 

Timestamps are an integral part of the Cassandra storage model. For each stored cell, the timestamp of the write operation is kept. The timestamp can be specified when a write operation is issued. If no timestamp is specified, the coordinator adds its own. The timestamp is usually propagated down to the replica and used to resolve data taints.

With this you can set at the moment of insert time to handle multiple versions of data. You do not need to write any custom code.
https://docs.datastax.com/en/cassandra/3.x/cassandra/dml/dml_write.html#dml_write_timestamp

A: Cassandra does support the concept of time-to-live (TTL) on columns. However, this mechanism treats the lifetime of a column (not a 'version' as you might associate with other systems). 
Additionally, Cassandra's timestamp, once you've inserted a record, is fixed (unless you perform an update operation). The timestamp serves as a clues about when the data has been inserted into the Cassandra cluster as a whole.
However, you might want to consider a different approach such as 'Event Sourcing' if you want to keep track of multiple versions of data across time. In event sourcing, every state change (or event) is stored as a separate object that records who made the change, when it was made, and the change itself. 
In this case, instead of just a data column, each state would have a list of events which are effectively versioned in cassandra.

A: Cassandra does allow you to store multiple versions of the same data but it doesn't provide a native mechanism out of the box to retrieve older versions of the data once they are stored. One would have to write custom code to achieve this functionality.
Cassandra has the timestamps feature which essentially allows you to keep copies of older versions of data by using those timestamps as a 'version' identifier.
But to fully handle multiple versions of a data you might want to consider using something other than just Cassandra or leverage a framework that provides advanced data versioning directly on top of Cassandra such as Apache Kafka where you could use Kafka Connect Cassandra Sink to store previous state of data.

A: Cassandra doesn't natively support 'multiple versions of data' as a feature unlike other Key-Value stores, but you can--------------------	    // and @@@ index of x in old array <= new arrListCount or index of x in new array is the number of x in arraylist. (new or old arrays)

            PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListCount); 
        
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            arrList.Insert(index, x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

            //Assert the index of x is not more than count
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListCount);
            //Assert whether every insertion operation maintain the order of elements in the ArrayList
            PexAssert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= index && index <= New_arrListIndexOfX);

        }
    }
   
}

