How can I specify that the return value of the ArrayList.Add() method (New_Ret) has to be a unique value of the list?
To make it clear, New_Ret is supposed to be the new count of the ArrayList when a new item is added. Is there any attribute or annotation I can use to specify this?
Please let me know if you need further information.

A: This type of property inferred from return values is currently not supported in Pex.
Pex generally infers properties about method inputs and outputs. Some properties that can be inferred include the return value and parameters' values. However, it doesn't infer properties from an object after a method call, such as what the new Count value looks like.
There are several ways to work around this:


*

*You may use an arbitrary but constant value (that would always hold) to test your method. 

*You may add extra data fields in your test class to keep track of the state. 

*You can test the addition logic separately and ensure that the Count property changes accordingly.


For example, 
[PexMethod]
public void Test_Add_Increases_Count([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList list, int value)
{
    int oldCount = list.Count;
    list.Add(value);
    int newCount = list.Count;
    Assert.That(newCount, Is.EqualTo(oldCount + 1));
}

If you see that your tests are not robust enough with Pex, consider asking/posting them on CodeSmell forum. The Pex community would be more than happy to provide answers or help out on the CodeSmell. 
Microsoft Pex currently has restrictions and limitations and these are not discussed thoroughly on the community.

A: Unfortunately, Pex currently does not support inferring contracts on method calls which potentially change the state of an object (i.e., have side-effects). Pex works based on mutation of input values and checks assumptions/conditions before and after a method call is executed. It would be great if more such features could be added - that's why such discussions usually lead to enhancements. We can watch the community's requests and may consider additional features for future releases. For now, one workaround could be to use your own invariants to keep track of your state and make sure that things are what you expect them to be.

A: Just to add onto the previous answer, you can also consider the use of the PexSymbolicValue attribute. This allows you to annotate properties that Pex will treat as symbolic. That means Pex will assign each value independently instead of treating them as the same constant. For more information and examples, you can look at the blog post here: https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/magazine/dn893315.aspx
Another workaround could be to mock out the ArrayList, giving it a fixed set of known behavior. This can provide a lot more control over the specific behaviors and can be especially useful if your testing focuses more on the logic within the ArrayList specifically instead of on how it interacts with your code.
In all these cases, you will have to find a way to make sure the contract you want to test is met through your concrete implementation.

A: If this is particularly important to you, you might want to make feature requests on the Pex User Voice page. If there have been many requests for this kind of feature it might be prioritized in future updates.
The other options are to just write your own tests (like the blog post shows) or to create a test case that only ensures the ArrayList indeed behaves as you would expect from your code or specific use cases.
That is, in an ideal world, Pex would provide a solution out-of-the-box with no need for developers to make customizations. But in practical terms, it will likely be the case, and these things will have to be tweaked as required.

A: If you need such an inferred property or property inferred from return value, it would be good to make a suggestion or feedback that you found this limitation confusing or not useful for your testing scenarios. You can provide a detailed description about your test scenario and express your opinion about why it is important for Pex to support such feature. Microsoft usually accepts user feedback and plans their features according to that. If your feedback is useful, it might end up being one of the enhancement requests.
More Pex-related discussions and suggestions usually take place on the Pex Forum at Microsoft, so posting there might also be helpful.
And as always, testing by design is a good approach to achieve a high code coverage and high quality--------------------            if(Old_arrListIndexOfX < New_arrListCount-1) 
            {
               throw new ExternalException("@@@");
            }
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ index of x in old array is leq plus 1 
